@page "/"

<PageTitle>Linq2Dashboard · faceted exploration for .NET</PageTitle>

<section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-inner">
        <div class="hero-text">
            <a class="hero-badge" href="agents"><span class="hero-badge-tag">New</span>Ask your dashboard with an AI agent<span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>

            <h1>Faceted exploration for any <span class="hero-gradient">.NET collection</span></h1>

            <p class="hero-lead">
                Facets with counts, metrics and rows that all update together on every click. The faceted search of an
                e-commerce site, over your own in-memory data, with a LINQ-flavoured API and Blazor components on top.
            </p>

            <div class="hero-actions">
                <a class="button" href="demo">Open the live demo <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
                <a class="button secondary" href="five-minutes">Start in five minutes</a>
            </div>

            <div class="code-block hero-install">
                <pre><code>dotnet add package Linq2Dashboard.Blazor --prerelease</code></pre>
                @((MarkupString)CodeLanguages.CopyButton)
            </div>

            <dl class="hero-stats">
                <div><dt>1M rows</dt><dd>built in about a second</dd></div>
                <div><dt>5–20 ms</dt><dd>per click, measured</dd></div>
                <div><dt>0</dt><dd>dependencies in the core</dd></div>
            </dl>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-demo">
            <div class="hero-window">
                <div class="hero-window-bar">
                    <span class="hero-window-dots" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
                    <span class="hero-window-title">@HeroRows.ToString("N0", System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture) orders · running in your browser</span>
                </div>
                <div class="hero-window-body">
                    @if (dashboard is null)
                    {
                        <div class="hero-skeleton" aria-label="Building the dashboard">
                            <div class="hero-skeleton-tiles"><i></i><i></i></div>
                            <div class="hero-skeleton-columns"><i></i><i></i></div>
                        </div>
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <DashboardView T="SampleOrder" Dashboard="dashboard" Formatter="DemoFormatter.Instance">
                            <div class="hero-metrics">
                                <Metric Key="@SampleOrderKeys.Orders" Name="Orders">
                                    <MetricTemplate Context="tile"><DemoTile Content="tile" Icon="cart" /></MetricTemplate>
                                </Metric>
                                <Metric Key="@SampleOrderKeys.Revenue">
                                    <MetricTemplate Context="tile"><DemoTile Content="tile" Icon="banknote" /></MetricTemplate>
                                </Metric>
                            </div>
                            <div class="hero-facets">
                                <ValueFacet For="x => x.Status">
                                    <ValueTemplate Context="value"><DemoStatus Value="value" /></ValueTemplate>
                                </ValueFacet>
                                <RangeFacet For="x => x.Amount" />
                            </div>
                        </DashboardView>
                    }
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="hero-demo-hint">This is the library, not a picture. Click a status or a bar.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
    <div class="home-section-head">
        <p class="home-eyebrow">What you get</p>
        <h2>Answers that add up, on every click</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="features">
        <div class="feature">
            <span class="feature-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6h18M7 12h10M10 18h4" /></svg></span>
            <h3>Facets that explain themselves</h3>
            <p>Every value shows how many rows it would give you. A facet's own selection is left out of its own counts, so picking Sweden never zeroes out Norway.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature">
            <span class="feature-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" /><path d="M5.6 18.4L18.4 5.6" /></svg></span>
            <h3>Null is a value</h3>
            <p>Missing values are shown, counted and selectable like any other. Filtered counts always add up to the rows in context.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature">
            <span class="feature-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13 2L3 14h9l-1 8 10-12h-9l1-8z" /></svg></span>
            <h3>Fast at a million rows</h3>
            <p>Columnar indexes and a single counting pass per facet. About a second to build, 5 to 20 ms per click on a server.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature">
            <span class="feature-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 2L2 7l10 5 10-5-10-5zM2 17l10 5 10-5M2 12l10 5 10-5" /></svg></span>
            <h3>No UI in the core</h3>
            <p>The engine is a pure function from selections to state. The Blazor package renders that state and never counts anything itself.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature">
            <span class="feature-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3l1.9 5.8L20 11l-6.1 2.2L12 19l-1.9-5.8L4 11l6.1-2.2L12 3z" /></svg></span>
            <h3>Ask it with an agent</h3>
            <p>The AI package turns a dashboard into tools an agent calls: describe, query and show. Its answers come from the same facets, so they agree with the screen.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feature">
            <span class="feature-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M19 21l-7-5-7 5V5a2 2 0 012-2h10a2 2 0 012 2z" /></svg></span>
            <h3>Bookmarkable</h3>
            <p>Selections serialise to JSON or a readable URL query, so a filtered view can be stored, shared and restored. The demo keeps its selections in the URL.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="home-section home-code">
    <div class="home-code-text">
        <p class="home-eyebrow">How it works</p>
        <h2>Define it once. Click forever.</h2>
        <p>
            Build a dashboard from the collection you already have. Facets, metrics and the sort order are fixed at that
            point; only the selections change afterwards, and the same selections always give the same state.
        </p>
        <ul class="home-checks">
            <li>Value, range, date, boolean, text and multi-valued facets</li>
            <li>Count, sum, average, distinct and calculated metrics</li>
            <li>Rows for the grid you already use, QuickGrid included</li>
            <li>Styled through <code>--l2d-*</code> custom properties</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="button secondary" href="getting-started">Read the guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </div>
    <div class="home-code-samples">
        <CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@BuildExample" />
        <CodeBlock Language="razor" Code="@RenderExample" />
    </div>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
    <div class="home-band">
        <div>
            <h2>Runs where .NET runs</h2>
            <p>
                This site is Blazor WebAssembly: every dashboard on it is generated, built and recalculated in your browser
                with the same library a server uses. Blazor Server is the primary target for large datasets. How it behaves
                and how it is built are both written down, and rendered here from the repository.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="home-band-actions">
            <a class="button" href="demo">Try the demo</a>
            <a class="button secondary" href="concept">Read the concept</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
